  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 239 PM EDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nThru tonight...Upper air sounding from XMR this morning showed\nnoticeably drier air through the column with PWAT down to 1.8\"\nwith the bulk of that moisture below 900 mb. Atlc sea breeze is \npushing inland sparking isolated showers and some of these will \ndevelop into lightning storms over the interior as boundary \ncollisions increase. The drier air aloft is producing high DCAPE \nvalues over 1100 J/kg so any deep convection will be capable of \ngusty winds up to 50 mph but coverage looks limited (~30%). \nConvection will wind down over the far interior by 10 pm.\n\nTue-Sun...High pressure ridge aloft moves in and holds across the\narea. The assocd suppression/subsidence as well as another plume \nof Saharan air will contribute to dry mid levels and below normal \nstorm chances. While skies may become more hazy this week due to \nthe Saharan dust, the particulates are suspended mostly aloft and \ndo not typically cause air quality issues at the sfc here in \ncentral FL. There is a gradual rise in rain chances Thu-Fri as \nmoisture/convergence pools on the north side of the low level \nridge axis. Then drier air moves back in this weekend and limits \nrain chances, especially Sunday. Any storms that do manage to form\nwill have the potential to become strong, producing strong wind \ngusts of 40-50 mph and frequent lightning strikes. Weak steering \nwinds will also allow for heavy downpours from some storms, \nproducing locally heavy rainfall of 1-2\" each day. \n\nThese drier than normal conditions will allow for rising heat \nimpacts through the week as little relief will be found from \nafternoon convection. Max temps will reach the low to mid 90s \neach day. Peak heat index values will easily reach 102-107F, and \nwill have to monitor the need for additional Heat Advisories as \nportions of the area should see heat index values rise to \n108-110F. Warm and muggy overnight temps in the mid to upper 70s \nwill also amplify the overall heat impacts, with widespread \nModerate to Major HeatRisk forecast for much of the area each day.\nContinuing to follow heat safety practices will remain important \nas we go through the week.",
